      "usable_content": "[{'content': 'Table 3 entry for Ref 124 reports Propionic acid in plasma/faecal samples associated with ICI (nivolumab/pembrolizumab) response in solid tumours (R n=15, NR n=37), with Univariate HR=0.08 (0.03–0.20) and Multivariate HR=0.07 (0.03–0.19).', 'relevance': 'Provides statistical association (HR) between a specific SCFA and ICI response in human samples, partially meeting the statistical association criterion.'}, {'content': 'Section 2.1.5 describes Inosine produced by Akkermansia muciniphila and Bifidobacterium pseudolongum enhancing ICB efficacy in mouse models (CRC, bladder, melanoma) via A2AR-cAMP-PKA pathway on T cells.', 'relevance': 'Describes the mechanism and microbial source of inosine, but explicitly limits the efficacy evidence to mouse models, not human quantification.'}, {'content': \"Section 5.2 ('Limitations of microbial metabolite research') states that 'reliable methods are still lacking to accurately identify the primary sources of specific metabolites' and 'relying solely on relative quantification can lead to inaccurate conclusions, making the incorporation of absolute quantification techniques essential'.\", 'relevance': 'Explicitly confirms the field-wide gap regarding absolute quantification and microbial source tracing in human studies.'}] This study quantified absolute concentrations of fecal short-chain fatty acids (acetate, propionate, butyrate) and serum metabolites (α-ketoglutaric acid, N-acetyl-L-glutamic acid, pyridoxine) in a mouse colorectal cancer model using LC-MS. It reported statistical correlations between these metabolite levels and tumor volume/survival outcomes under anti-PD-1 therapy. The authors demonstrated a mechanistic pathway where probiotic modulation of gut microbes increased SCFAs, which correlated with increased serum immunomodulatory metabolites and enhanced T cell infiltration in the tumor microenvironment. Causal links were supported by fecal microbiota and supernatant transplan